@[edit-confs step]
(Edit with @[$EDITOR FILE] (nano); other editors: @[help editors])

Partitions and mounts: @[$ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/fstab]
Add a line per filesystem below "Add your file systems here" (@[lsblk -f]
shows your partitions); e.g. for a UEFI disk with an ext4 root:
  /dev/nvme0n1p2  /          ext4  defaults  0 1
  /dev/nvme0n1p1  /boot/efi  vfat  defaults  0 2
Fields: device, mount point, filesystem, options, dump (0), fsck order (1 for
the root filesystem, 2 for others, 0 for btrfs and swap). The file itself has
commented examples for ext4, XFS, btrfs subvolumes, the EFI partition, swap
and /tmp in RAM; more in @[guide disks] (also zram).

Also, take a look at the files in @[$ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/sysconfig/] and edit
any you feel should be different. Especially, you should make sure the
@[facilities] file looks correct, and if you selected a custom keymap at the
start of this install, you'll need to edit the file called @[keymap] in that
directory (type @[cat $ENCHANT_TMP/keymap] to see which you chose).

Hostname: @[$ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/hostname]
If you wish to set a hostname for your system, add it here.

Networking: @[$ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/network/interfaces]
Read at boot by ifup (@[man interfaces]); the file has commented examples for
wired DHCP, Wi-Fi and a fixed address. Interface names: @[ip link] (they are
the same on the installed system). E.g. Wi-Fi, after copying the WPA config:
  @[cp /etc/wpa_supplicant.conf $ENCHANT_TARGET/etc/]
  auto wlan0
  iface wlan0 inet dhcp
          pre-up wpa_supplicant -B -i wlan0 -c /etc/wpa_supplicant.conf

Timezone: (replace "SOME/TIMEZONE" with your actual timezone)
  @[enchantment chroot ln -sf /usr/share/zoneinfo/SOME/TIMEZONE /etc/localtime]

Type @[next] to go to the next step.
